  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nThis afternoon, a surface ridge of high pressure will build into the \narea, as the cold front that brought storms to the area last \nnight into this morning finally sinks south of the CWA. This is \nprovided much more comfortable temperatures with highs in the \nupper 70s to lower 80s. The surface ridge will remain over the area \ntonight providing light winds and mostly clear skies. That coupled \nwith a moist boundary layer will allow fog to develop over most of \nthe CWA. The best potential for dense fog will be across central \nMissouri along and east of the Highway 65 corridor overnight into \ntomorrow morning. Low temperatures are forecast to drop into the \nupper 50s to mid 60s. Tomorrow afternoon, the surface ridge will \nbegin to move east of the area will modest WAA increasing. This will \nallow highs to rise into the low to mid 80s. Saturday, the local \narea will be under quasi-zonal flow aloft. A few shortwaves are \nforecast to move into the area while will provide a few chances for \nstorms. The more vigorous of these shortwave is progged to move \nthrough the area during the afternoon and evening hours in a \nenvironment of 1500-2500J/Kg of MUCAPE and 30-40kts of 0-6Km shear \nwhich will be capable of producing strong storms capable of large \nhail and damaging winds. Additional shortwave will continue to move \ninto the area on zonal flow aloft through Sunday continuing shower \nand thunderstorm chances. With PWATs in the 1.50-1.75\" range and \nseveral rounds of storms possible, localized flooding may be \npossible. Highs Saturday and Sunday will be in the 80s.\n\nSunday night and Monday look to be mainly dry across the area as the \nlocal area falls under the periphery of an upper level ridge that \nwill become more amplified over the southeastern CONUS. This will \nallow temperatures to warm into to the mid 80s to lower 90s on \nMonday. Monday night into Tuesday a upper level trough will move \nfrom the northern Plains into the northern Great Lakes. This feature \nwill have a two pronged effect...first, it will flatten the upper \nlevel ridge across the region. Secondly, it will force an attendant \nslow moving cold front into the area. Current model solutions \nsuggest this front will stall over the area and be the focus for \nseveral rounds of showers and thunderstorms to develop over the area \non Monday night through Wednesday as additional shortwaves move \nthrough the area on zonal flow aloft. The temperature spread on \nTuesday and Wednesday is expected to be wide with the cold front \ndraped across the forecast area. Highs on Tuesday are forecast to be \nin the mid 70s over the northwest CWA but in the upper 80s to near \n90 south. Wednesday, highs will range from near 70 north to the \nmid 80s south.|",
